="pubmed">16985051</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang J., Stevens M.F., Laughton C.A., Madhusudan S., Bradshaw T.D. Acquired resistance to temozolomide in glioma cell lines: molecular mechanisms and potential translational applications. Oncology. 2010;78:103&#x2013;114.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20357518</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Oliva C.R., Nozell S.E., Diers A. Acquisition of temozolomide chemoresistance in gliomas leads to remodeling of mitochondrial electron transport chain. J Biol Chem. 2010;285:39759&#x2013;39767.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3000957</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20870728</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lee E.S., Ko K.K., Joe Y.A., Kang S.G., Hong Y.K. Inhibition of STAT3 reverses drug resistance acquired in temozolomide-resistant human glioma cells. Oncol Lett. 2011;2:115&#x2013;121.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3412536</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">22870139</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ueno H., Tomiyama A., Yamaguchi H. Augmentation of invadopodia formation in temozolomide-resistant or adopted glioma is regulated by c-Jun terminal kinase-paxillin axis. Biochem Biophys Res Commun. 2015;468:240&#x2013;247.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26518652</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wang X., Jia L., Jin X. NF-&#x3ba;B inhibitor reverses temozolomide resistance in human glioma TR/U251 cells. Oncol Lett. 2015;9:2586&#x2013;2590.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4473369</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26137111</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>McFaline-Figueroa J.L., Braun C.J., Stanciu M. Minor changes in expression of the mismatch repair protein MSH2 exert a major impact on glioblastoma response to temozolomide. Cancer Res. 2015;75:3127&#x2013;3138.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4526337</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26025730</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Banelli B., Carra E., Barbieri F. The histone demethylase KDM5A is a key factor for the resistance to temozolomide in glioblastoma. Cell Cycle. 2015;14:3418&#x2013;3429.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4825557</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26566863</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Giannini C., Sarkaria J.N., Saito A. Patient tumor EGFR and PDGFRA gene amplifications retained in an invasive intracranial xenograft model of glioblastoma multiforme. Neuro Oncol. 2005;7:164&#x2013;176.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC1871885</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">15831234</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Clarke M.J., Mulligan E.A., Grogan P.T. Effective sensitization of temozolomide by ABT-888 is lost with development of temozolomide resistance in glioblastoma xenograft lines. Mol Cancer Ther. 2009;8:407&#x2013;414.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2692390</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19174557</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kitange G.J., Carlson B.L., Schroeder M.A. Induction of MGMT expression is associated with temozolomide resistance in glioblastoma xenografts. Neuro Oncol. 2009;11:281&#x2013;291.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2718972</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18952979</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hunn M.K., Bauer E., Wood C.E. Dendritic cell vaccination combined with temozolomide retreatment: results of a phase I trial in patients with recurrent glioblastoma multiforme. J Neurooncol. 2015;121:319&#x2013;329.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">25366363</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Quinn J.A., Jiang S.X., Reardon D.A. Phase II trial of temozolomide plus O6-benzylguanine in adults with recurrent, temozolomide-resistant malignant glioma. J Clin Oncol. 2009;27:1262&#x2013;1267.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2667825</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19204199</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Dresemann G., Weller M., Rosenthal M.A. Imatinib in combination with hydroxyurea versus hydroxyurea alone as oral therapy in patients with progressive pretreated glioblastoma resistant to standard dose temozolomide. J Neurooncol. 2010;96:393&#x2013;402.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">19688297</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">28465464</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2017</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>18</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2018</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>13</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1540-9538</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>214</Volume><Issue>6</Issue><PubDate><Year>2017</Year><Month>Jun</Month><Day>05</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>The Journal of experimental medicine</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Exp Med</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Inflammatory signals from photoreceptor modulate pathological retinal angiogenesis via c-Fos.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1753</StartPage><EndPage>1767</EndPage><MedlinePgn>1753-1767</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1084/jem.20161645</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Pathological neovessels growing into the normally avascular photoreceptors cause vision loss in many eye diseases, such as age-related macular degeneration and macular telangiectasia. Ocular neovascularization is strongly associated with inflammation, but the source of inflammatory signals and the mechanisms by which these signals regulate the disruption of avascular privilege in photoreceptors are unknown. In this study, we found that c-Fos, a master inflammatory regulator, was increased in photoreceptors in a model of pathological blood vessels invading photoreceptors: the very low-density lipoprotein receptor-deficient (<i>Vldlr<sup>-/-</sup></i> ) mouse. Increased c-Fos induced inflammatory cytokines interleukin 6 (IL-6) and tumor necrosis factor (TNF), leading to activation of signal transducer and activator of transcription 3 (STAT3) and increased TNF&#x3b1;-induced protein 3 (TNFAIP3) in <i>Vldlr<sup>-/-</sup></i> photoreceptors. IL-6 activated the STAT3/vascular endothelial growth factor A (VEGFA) pathway directly, and elevated TNFAIP3 suppressed SOCS3 (suppressor of cytokine signaling 3)-activated STAT3/VEGFA indirectly. Inhibition of c-Fos using photoreceptor-specific <i>AAV</i> (adeno-associated virus)-<i>hRK</i> (human rhodopsin kinase)-<i>sh_c-fos</i> or a chemical inhibitor substantially reduced the pathological neovascularization and rescued visual function in <i>Vldlr<sup>-/-</sup></i> mice. These findings suggested that the photoreceptor c-Fos controls blood vessel growth into the normally avascular photoreceptor layer through the inflammatory signal-induced STAT3/VEGFA pathway.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2017 Sun et al.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sun</LastName><ForeName>Ye</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7674-9056</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lin</LastName><ForeName>Zhiqiang</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Cardiology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liu</LastName><ForeName>Chi-Hsiu</ForeName><Initials>CH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gong</LastName><ForeName>Yan</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-4805-0459</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liegl</LastName><ForeName>Raffael</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fredrick</LastName><ForeName>Thomas W</ForeName><Initials>TW</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Meng</LastName><ForeName>Steven S</ForeName><Initials>SS</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-5585-5669</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Burnim</LastName><ForeName>Samuel B</ForeName><Initials>SB</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-5452-6743</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Zhongxiao</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Akula</LastName><ForeName>James D</ForeName><Initials>JD</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-8049-1812</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pu</LastName><ForeName>William T</ForeName><Initials>WT</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Cardiology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Harvard Stem Cell Institute, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Jing</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Smith</LastName><ForeName>Lois E H</ForeName><Initials>LEH</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-7644-6410</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ophthalmology, Harvard Medical School, Boston Children's Hospital, Boston, MA 02115 lois.smith@childrens.harvard.edu.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>R01 EY017017</GrantID><Acronym>EY</Acronym><Agency>NEI NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant><Grant><GrantID>P30 HD018655</GrantID><Acronym>HD</Acronym><Agency>NICHD NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant><Grant><GrantID>R01 EY024963</GrantID><Acronym>EY</Acronym><Agency>NEI NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant><Grant><GrantID>U54 HD090255</GrantID><Acronym>HD</Acronym><Agency>NICHD NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant><Grant><GrantID>R24 EY024864</GrantID><Acronym>EY</Acronym><Agency>NEI